# The `[not yet]` markers that quote NO ERROR CODE, and the whole of what "every unbuilt
# form names the rule that refuses it" means as a criterion (#86). One `path<TAB>slug` per
# line, where the slug is the marker's first seven significant words — see
# scripts/marker-codes-check.sh for why the key is a slug and not a line number.
#
# IT IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE CRITERION MET rather than the file being unused. It opened
# with what #86 measured and closed in one pass, four ways and no others. Most markers simply
# gained the code of the rule that refuses them, each read off the COMPILER rather than off
# the prose beside it. Some were the marker system describing itself and moved to
# docs/.marker-prose, which is not a marker to answer for. Three named forms this compiler
# accepts in SILENCE — `##`, `del` of an owning value, a redundant `match` arm — and those
# name the ticket for the rule that does not exist (#93, #94, #95), because the debt there is
# a missing rule and not a missing citation. The rest were rewritten out of the marker
# altogether: `[not yet]` promises a clean compile error naming the form, and absent behaviour
# with no form to refuse cannot keep that promise however long it waits.
#
# The gate still reads it. A line added here is a marker somebody could not close today, and
# clause 3 takes it back off the day the marker gains a code — so the countdown cannot drift
# from the documents in either direction.
